      "faqs": [
        {
          "q": "What is the Best Time to Visit Ladakh?",
          "a": "- May to September – Ideal for travel, as roads to Nubra and [Pangong](https://indiatour.live/attractions/pangong-tso) are open, and the weather is pleasant.\n- October to April – Harsh winters with extreme cold, but a great time for the Chadar Trek and experiencing frozen landscapes."
        },
        {
          "q": "How Many Days Are Needed for a Ladakh Trip with Turtuk?",
          "a": "- 7-8 days – Covers Leh, [Nubra Valley](https://indiatour.live/attractions/nubra-valley), Pangong Lake, and [Turtuk](https://indiatour.live/attractions/turtuk-village) comfortably.\n- 5-6 days – Covers Leh, Nubra, and Pangong but might feel rushed.\n- 9-10 days – Allows more time for acclimatization and exploring offbeat places like [Tso Moriri](https://indiatour.live/attractions/tso-moriri-lake) and Hanle."
        },
        {
          "q": " Do I Need Permits for This Trip?",
          "a": "Yes, Inner Line Permits (ILP) are required for:\n- Nubra Valley\n- Pangong Lake\n- Turtuk Village\n- [Tso Moriri](https://indiatour.live/attractions/tso-moriri-lake)\n\n**Where to Get It?** Apply online or at the Leh DC office. Foreign nationals need a Protected Area Permit (PAP)."
        },
        {
          "q": "Is Ladakh Safe for Solo or Female Travelers?",
          "a": "Yes, Ladakh is one of the safest destinations in India.\n- Locals are friendly and helpful.\n- Crime rates are very low.\n- Solo riders should stay connected and avoid riding alone at night."
        },
        {
          "q": "What Are the Road Conditions Like?",
          "a": "Expect all kinds of terrain:\n\n- **Leh to Nubra (via [Khardung La](https://indiatour.live/attractions/khardung-la))** – Steep climbs, rough patches, and unpredictable weather.\n- **Nubra to Pangong (via Shyok)** – Narrow roads, river crossings, and rocky stretches.\n- **Leh to Pangong (via Chang La)** – High-altitude pass with black ice in colder months.\n\nA 4x4 vehicle is recommended for self-drive trips."
        },
        {
          "q": "How Can I Avoid Altitude Sickness (AMS)?",
          "a": "Ladakh’s high altitude (11,500+ feet) can cause AMS. To prevent it:\n- Acclimatize for at least two days in Leh before heading to higher altitudes.\n- Drink plenty of water and eat light meals.\n- Avoid alcohol and smoking.\n- Take Diamox (after consulting a doctor).\n\nDon’t rush, as AMS can affect anyone, regardless of fitness level."
        },
        {
          "q": "What’s the Accommodation Like in Nubra, Pangong & Turtuk?",
          "a": "Stay options range from budget to premium:\n\n- **Leh & Nubra Valley** – Hotels, guesthouses, and homestays.\n- **Pangong Lake** – Tented camps or homestays at Spangmik village.\n- **Turtuk** – Only homestays available, offering an immersive local experience."
        },
        {
          "q": "What Should I Pack for a Ladakh Trip?",
          "a": "Ladakh’s weather is unpredictable. Pack:\n- Warm layers – Thermals, gloves, jackets, and woolen socks.\n- Sun protection – Sunglasses, sunscreen, and lip balm.\n- Rain gear – Sudden weather changes are common.\n- Power bank and offline maps – Limited electricity and no network at Pangong."
        }
      ]
